<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Torc</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as torq, torque, torques</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A torc is a large rigid neck ring of metal, made as a single piece or from twisted strands and usually open at the front, worn in the Scythian, Illyrian, Thracian, Celtic and other cultures of the European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> from around the 8th century BCE to the 3rd century CE. Among the Iron Age <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> the gold torc seems to have been a key object, marking its wearer as a person of high rank: apparently usually a woman until the 3rd century BCE, and thereafter usually, though not only, a man. Celtic gods were shown wearing or holding torcs, and many of the finest works of ancient Celtic art are torcs, such as the Snettisham Torc. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> took them up as decorations for distinguished soldiers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/torc.jpg" alt="Torc" width="330" height="285" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vassil · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Torque_à_tampons_Somme-Suippe_Musée_Saint-Remi_120208.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…with coral and enamel; the gold-covered helmet from Agris; the Battersea Shield, the Witham Shield and the horned Waterloo Helmet from British rivers; bronze mirrors engraved with spirals; and gold torcs, the neck rings worn by nobles and shown on gods, such as the Great <b>Torc</b> from Snettisham.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>Celebrated pieces include the Basse Yutz Flagons, the gold-covered Agris Helmet, the Battersea Shield and the Great <b>Torc</b> from Snettisham.</blockquote><a href="/culture/la-tene-culture">La Tène culture · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li></ul></section>
